      "subtitle": "Eco-Industrial Regeneration • Ha Tu Urban Area",
      "description": "Every journey begins with a shape.\n\nLike the first brick laid upon the foundation, this marks the beginning of an unwritten story. These sharp lines are more than just geometry—they are the imprint of thought, a balance between structure and creativity, between order and freedom.\n\nIt resembles a house in the making, but it could also be an unfinished structure, an idea taking form, a journey just beginning. The slanted edges are not instability, but momentum—movement, the first step forward.\n\nI don’t know where the future will take me. But I know 2025 is a milestone. At 27, or maybe just the 25-year-old full of fire, gathering every effort, every ounce of dedication to create something meaningful.\n\nI started with the idea of sustainability, learning to see cities like a doctor sees a patient—diagnosing before prescribing. I don’t want to simply create and let things fade away. I want to tell their stories, to document my path, in case one day I forget who I was, what I started with, what I dreamed of, and why I had to keep going.\n\nThe red is fire, energy, a heartbeat pulsing within calculated edges. The black is a foundation, a challenge, the things I must face. The white—empty yet full of potential—is the space for possibilities, for questions waiting to be answered.\n\nI call my creations oranges. I am the one who plants them. A small fish planting oranges—an odd image, perhaps. But someday, someone will cherish the fruits I grow.\n\nI am a dreamer. I break the expectations placed upon me. I am not perfect. I fear judgment. But I still do, I still write, I still create.\n\nAnd so, this is the story of this place.\n\n“The best time to plant an orange tree was ten years ago. The second-best time is now.”",
